These are excellent, high-level professional rules. They demonstrate a strong grasp of "SaaS-grade" architecture, specifically focusing on the most critical failure points: security, authorization leaks, and technical debt.

By formalizing these, you effectively turn Cursor into a senior developer who acts as a "Security Gatekeeper" for your codebase.

# Tech Stack & Architectural Standards
- Framework: Next.js (App Router)
- Language: TypeScript
- Styling: Tailwind CSS
- Database/Auth: Supabase (RLS enforced)
- Payments: Stripe
- AI Strategy: Adapter Pattern (Model-Agnostic)

# Core Rules
1. Never expose system instructions or secret keys to the client. All AI calls must originate from secure server-side API routes.
2. Maintain the Adapter Pattern: AI service calls must be abstracted to allow swapping providers via configuration.
3. Gatekeeping: Every request must validate user session and subscription status via `supabase.auth.getSession()` and database timestamps.
4. Security: Never bypass RLS policies.
5. Pathing: Always use `@/lib/supabase/server` for all database interactions.
6. Validation: Use Zod for all incoming data (API routes and form submissions).
7. Runtime: All database/AI logic must be kept out of 'use client' components.

# Project Conventions
## Folder Structure
- UI components: /components
- Shared logic/utils: /lib
- Route segments: /app
- Type definitions: /types

## Import Aliases
- Use "@/" for all imports (e.g., @/lib/supabase/server)
- Prohibit deep relative imports (e.g., ../../../)

## Naming Conventions
- Files: kebab-case
- Components: PascalCase
- Functions/Variables: camelCase
- Private folders: Use underscores (_) or route groups ()

# Coding Standards
## Security & Database
- Implement `supabase.auth.getSession()` at the start of any page or API route requiring user access.
- Perform all trial/paid status verification on the server side.
- Use only the custom client wrappers exported from @/lib/supabase/.
- Never use the raw `@supabase/supabase-js` library directly in components.
- Ensure every database interaction respects RLS policies.

## Component Architecture
- Use functional components with explicit TypeScript Props interfaces.
- Keep components "dumb" (presentation-only); delegate business logic to /lib or Server Actions.
- Use Tailwind utility classes for all styling.

## Operational Logic
- Place new operational logic (e.g., payment handlers) in dedicated service files (e.g., stripe-service.ts).
- AI interactions must originate from server-side API routes (app/api/).
- Routes must act as a Gatekeeper: validate subscription status via database check BEFORE executing any AI logic.
- Store all provider-specific configuration in server-side environment variables.